    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 38,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 975,
      "width": 596.7642822265625,
      "height": 360,
      "strokeColor": "#ffa657",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "text": "🔍 Critical design decisions\n\n1. **Token-ID trie vs character trie** — Most constrained decoding uses character-level grammars.\n   This project uses token-ID-level: edges in the trie are token IDs (integers), not characters.\n   This means we don't need to decode/encode during generation — just compare ints.\n\n2. **Constrained decoding guarantees correctness** — The model can ONLY generate tokens that\n   lead to a valid function name. It literally cannot output \"fn_add_numers\" (typo).\n   This is stronger than post-hoc validation (which would just reject bad output).\n\n3. **No LLM for parameters** — This is the performance win. Parameter extraction is pure regex.\n   A single forward pass selects the function; parameters come from deterministic patterns.\n   This means 11 prompts cost ~11 partial forward passes (not 11 full generations).\n\n4. **`_tensor_to_ids` handles HF quirks** — HuggingFace returns different shapes depending\n   on batch size, device, etc. This helper normalizes everything to list[int].\n\n5. **`_select_max_logit` implements the constraint** — The 'allowed' set comes from\n   trie.children.keys(). Only those token IDs are considered. Non-allowed tokens\n   get -inf effectively. This is the \"gate\" that enforces the constraint.\n\n6. **TypedDict vs dataclass** — DecodedCall is a TypedDict for lightweight typing.\n   No __init__, no comparison methods, no serialization overhead. Just type hints.\n   TrieNode is a dataclass because it needs default factory and mutation.\n\n7. **Data-driven parameter extraction** — Originally _generate_parameters had hardcoded\n   branches for fn_greet and fn_substitute_string_with_regex. Now _extract_string_param()\n   uses **parameter name conventions** (source, regex, name, etc.) to dispatch regexes.\n   Peer review can add/rename functions without touching extraction code. The parameter\n   schema drives the logic, not the function name.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "id": "dvXS7ch3",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "elementType": "text",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🔍 Critical design decisions\n\n1. **Token-ID trie vs character trie** — Most constrained decoding uses character-level grammars.\n   This project uses token-ID-level: edges in the trie are token IDs (integers), not characters.\n   This means we don't need to decode/encode during generation — just compare ints.\n\n2. **Constrained decoding guarantees correctness** — The model can ONLY generate tokens that\n   lead to a valid function name. It literally cannot output \"fn_add_numers\" (typo).\n   This is stronger than post-hoc validation (which would just reject bad output).\n\n3. **No LLM for parameters** — This is the performance win. Parameter extraction is pure regex.\n   A single forward pass selects the function; parameters come from deterministic patterns.\n   This means 11 prompts cost ~11 partial forward passes (not 11 full generations).\n\n4. **`_tensor_to_ids` handles HF quirks** — HuggingFace returns different shapes depending\n   on batch size, device, etc. This helper normalizes everything to list[int].\n\n5. **`_select_max_logit` implements the constraint** — The 'allowed' set comes from\n   trie.children.keys(). Only those token IDs are considered. Non-allowed tokens\n   get -inf effectively. This is the \"gate\" that enforces the constraint.\n\n6. **TypedDict vs dataclass** — DecodedCall is a TypedDict for lightweight typing.\n   No __init__, no comparison methods, no serialization overhead. Just type hints.\n   TrieNode is a dataclass because it needs default factory and mutation.\n\n7. **Data-driven parameter extraction** — Originally _generate_parameters had hardcoded\n   branches for fn_greet and fn_substitute_string_with_regex. Now _extract_string_param()\n   uses **parameter name conventions** (source, regex, name, etc.) to dispatch regexes.\n   Peer review can add/rename functions without touching extraction code. The parameter\n   schema drives the logic, not the function name.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "versionNonce": 1609324507,
      "index": "aG",
      "isDeleted": false,
